 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157135 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $4e^x - 5 >  0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5b64c26-300d-530d-b22f-ff061f4e3296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157135 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{x}-5 & >0 \\\\ 4{e}^{x} & >5 \\\\ {e}^{x} & >\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\ x & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157136 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7660" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157136 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $3 \\cdot 5^x <   7^{3-x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157136 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95104c90-f452-5551-adec-836ef1192276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157136 #edb/ref 1074 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {5}^{x} & <{7}^{3-x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{3-x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <(3-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7) & <3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157142 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157142 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $5 \\cdot \\left( \\dfrac{3}{4} \\right)^x + 2 \\geq 12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157142 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c336514-aac7-5f9c-8118-8a16188f2d42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157142 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 10 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x} & \\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 3 - \\ln 4$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157143 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157143 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $4 \\cdot 3^{8-x} + 12 > 0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "185e4c00-f218-5d54-8345-397267a74b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157143 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{8-x}+12 & >0 \\\\ 4\\cdot {3}^{8-x} & >-12 \\\\ {3}^{8-x} & >-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n3^{8-x} > 0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $3^{8-x} > -3$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157429 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157429 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157429 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157429 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050649 #edb/re