6250359 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250359 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250359 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250359 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}\\le \\frac{1}{9}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250359 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9725f40a-ccc5-5b84-9193-a09e8482b8d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250359 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{9} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1} & \\le \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x+1}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ (x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ (x+1)(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\le -\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\ge \\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250364 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4>-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ced4ffe4-e7bc-5404-a238-528823539017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250364 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4 & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5} & >-6 \\\\ (0.43)^{4x+5} & >-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause a power of a positive number is always greater than $0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>0\n$$\n\nfor all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the inequality $(0.43)^{4x+5}>-4$ is satisfied for all real values of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250368 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186250038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250368 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115248" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250368 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250368 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality ${7}^{x+1}>{5}^{x-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250368 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dea1588-01f3-5e0d-9811-784ff06fa34a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250368 #edb/ref 1074 "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{x+1} & >{5}^{x-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{x+1}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x-1}) \\\\ (x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 & >x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5) & >-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7 \\\\ x & >-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡7-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293771 #edb/ref 1080 ">" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293771 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293771 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d80fb632-82ac-5a2d-82ff-8e718314f84a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293846 #edb/ref 1080 "{log}_{4}(14)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186293846 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498